      "id": "MJUe",
      "code_hash": "42d48b6717eb15a98fab051035388ebc",
      "outputs": [
        {
          "type": "data",
          "data": {
            "text/markdown": "<span class=\"markdown prose dark:prose-invert contents\"><h1 id=\"derivatives-and-finite-differences\">Derivatives and Finite Differences</h1>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Eight functions, each in its own tab, accessible in any order at any time.\nFor each one:</span>\n<ol>\n<li>Start at <code>x = target</code>, where the slider begins (its own position isn't\n   graded).</li>\n<li>Fill in <code>dy/dx</code> at every point in the table under the plot \u2014 all 5\n   points are graded independently.</li>\n<li>Move the slider around too, if you want to see more points than the\n   table's 5 \u2014 each one you visit gets plotted on the main plot as well.</li>\n<li>Select the function that fits the plot as close as possible (and is\n   probably the derivative).</li>\n</ol>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">There's no live right/wrong feedback \u2014 only the actual submission is\ngraded. The slider's own position is never checked \u2014 what's graded is\nwhether <code>dy/dx</code> was ever entered correctly at each of the table's 5\npoints, regardless of where the slider ends up afterward.</span>\n<span class=\"paragraph\">Each tab is labeled with its internal key (e.g. <code>square</code>) so a submission\nreport entry like <code>square_deriv_value_0</code> (the table's leftmost point)\nthrough <code>square_deriv_value_4</code> (rightmost) can be matched back to the\nright tab and column. The report is pass/fail only \u2014 it doesn't reveal\nthe correct values.</span></span>"
          }
        }
      ],
      "console": []
    },
